---
/*
  The shell every page sits inside: the document head, the spine, the corner
  treatment, and the footer. Pages supply only their own content.

  EVERY PAGE IS PAPER (section 82). `theme` still exists and still sets
  data-theme, because the menu panel inverts against the page it covers — but no
  PAGE passes it, and setting it on one reopens an owner's decision. There is no
  interpolated state between the two polarities anywhere, and never was.
*/
import "../styles/global.css";
/*
  The icons live in src/, not public/, so the build hashes their filenames
  (section 74). Anything referencing them — here and the generated manifest —
  gets the hashed URL, and there is no unhashed copy left at a fixed path to
  serve a stale version behind everyone's back.
*/
import faviconUrl from "../assets/icons/favicon.svg?url";
import appleTouchIconUrl from "../assets/icons/apple-touch-icon.png?url";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import {getSiteSettings} from "../lib/content";
import type {OverlayPolarity} from "../lib/media";
import {pick} from "../lib/locales";
import {imageUrl} from "../lib/image";
import {LOCALES, localePath, switchLocalePath, type Locale} from "../lib/locales";
import {t} from "../i18n/ui";

interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
  /** Page title, without the brand name; the brand is appended here. */
  title?: string;
  description?: string;
  /*
    EVERY PAGE IS PAPER (2026-08-11, section 82). The OWNER decided it, having
    been told what it costs, and no page passes this prop any more.

    WHAT IT WAS, so nobody rebuilds it by accident. Sections 77 to 80 argued
    ENTER IN DARKNESS, BUY IN LIGHT: ink was the world (home, about, process,
    new, a drop's page), paper was the shop (the catalogue, a Creature, the
    order flow, contact), and the wash was the border between them, performed
    once on the only page that held both. It was a good argument and it is
    finished. Twenty-five theme="dark" declarations came out across six pages;
    the wash's two layers, its clip-path and its polarity props came out with
    them, because an edge travelling down a screen that looks identical on both
    sides of it is not a quiet gesture, it is nothing.

    WHAT THE BOUNDARY IS NOW: the same held screen at the same place, crossed by
    WRITING THE BRAND'S NAME with your own scroll. The mark was decorative on
    top of the inversion; it is the whole event now, and it speaks in his hand
    rather than in the site's voice. See BoundaryWash.

    WHAT SURVIVED, and it is not the same thing: POLARITY OVER PHOTOGRAPHY. A
    caption still takes the measured polarity of the pixels under it (`overlay`
    and `overlayCaption` per image). That has nothing to do with a page's own
    ground and is load-bearing for legibility; do not touch it while tidying
    this away. The chrome and the drawn mark no longer sit on photography at all
    (sections 87 and 96), so neither has a polarity to measure.

    SO WHAT IS THIS PROP FOR. A local inversion, and there is exactly one on the
    site: the menu panel, which inverts against the page it covers. If you are
    about to set it on a PAGE, you are reopening an owner's decision — take it
    to him rather than to this file.
  */
  theme?: "light" | "dark";
  /*
    THE OPENING FRAME'S OWN POLARITY, AND THE CHROME FLOATS ON IT (2026-08-12,
    section 115). A page that begins with a full-bleed photograph passes that
    photograph's measured `overlay` here; the chrome then takes zero height, the
    picture starts at pixel zero, and the marks are coloured for what they land
    on — which is what both reference sites do (section 107, `RO01`, `VW01`).

    A PAGE THAT DOES NOT OPEN ON A PHOTOGRAPH PASSES NOTHING, and gets the paper
    band section 87 built. That is the default and it is deliberately the
    default: floating has to be asked for, by a page that knows what is
    underneath it.

    This replaces the old `chrome` prop, which carried the same idea and was
    removed in section 87 along with the runtime observer that kept it in step.
    There is no observer now and there does not need to be: a page has one
    opening frame, known at build time, and the marks never travel past it.
  */
  chromeOver?: OverlayPolarity | null;
  /** Absolute URL of the sharing image. Always a real photograph, never art. */
  image?: string | null;
  /*
    The shipping line is SHOP dress (section 68). It opens every commerce
    surface, and it leaves the two culture pages: process and about are where
    a visitor enters the brand's world, and logistics before the first pixel
    of a world is how the spell breaks. Ferdressed, whose device the banner
    is, sets it under the wordmark; nobody crowns an about page with it.
  */
  shopChrome?: boolean;
}

const {
  locale,
  title,
  description: rawDescription,
  theme = "light",
  image,
  shopChrome = true,
  chromeOver = null,
} = Astro.props;

/*
  A description is a single line by the time it reaches a search result or a
  share card, so collapse it here rather than asking every page to remember.
  His copy carries meaningful line breaks on the page; they are not meaningful
  in a meta tag.
*/
const description = rawDescription?.replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim() || undefined;

const settings = await getSiteSettings();

const brand = "Aleksander Cecco";

/*
  TITLES AS A SYSTEM (2026-08-03), because a tab and a history entry are the two
  places a person meets this site without seeing any of its design.

  The shape is `Thing / Aleksander Cecco`, narrow to wide, so the distinguishing
  word is FIRST: a reader with six tabs open sees "Oblivion", "Gallery",
  "Corvinus", not six identical brand names truncated at the same point. The
  brand still closes every one, because the tab may be the only place a visitor
  who arrived from Instagram reads the name at all.

  A Creature adds its price, because that is the fact a person is most often
  tab-hoarding in order to compare, and it costs nothing to give it to them.
*/
const documentTitle = title ? `${title} / ${brand}` : brand;

const pathname = Astro.url.pathname;
const canonical = Astro.site ? new URL(pathname, Astro.site) : null;

const allowIndexing = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_ALLOW_INDEXING === "true";

const banner = pick(settings.shippingFree, locale);

const shareImage =
  image ?? (settings.openingMedia ? imageUrl(settings.openingMedia.poster, 1200) : null);
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang={locale} data-theme={theme}>
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{documentTitle}</title>
    {description && <meta name="description" content={description} />}

    {/*
      THE MARK IN THE TAB is the AC monogram EXTRACTED from the signature
      itself (section 72, superseding 71): the owner chose his actual hand
      over the redrawn candidate's easy legibility, and asked for the A alone
      — the 'l' that shares the A's continuous outline was cut away along a
      corridor measured by scanline. At 16px the mark reads as his gesture
      rather than as the letters, and he accepted exactly that trade. The
      redrawn candidate stays in docs/monogram/ as the recorded alternative.
      The SVG follows the reader's system theme; the full signature stays
      everywhere else.
    */}
    {/*
      CONTENT-HASHED, and that is the point (section 74). These were static
      files in public/ under fixed names, and a fixed name is a name that can
      go stale invisibly: the touch icon shipped broken and the manifest's
      512 icon shipped the OLD mark, through two rebuilds, because nothing
      downstream had any way to notice the bytes had changed. Imported, they
      carry Vite's content hash, so changing an icon changes its URL, every
      cache misses by construction, and an icon that no longer exists breaks
      the build instead of shipping quietly.
    */}
    {/*
      TWO SHAPES, DELIBERATELY (section 78).

      The hashed URLs are what this document DECLARES, and they keep every
      property section 74 bought: change an icon and its URL changes, so no
      cache anywhere can serve the old bytes.

      `/favicon.ico` is here because a hashed SVG is not enough in practice.
      iOS Safari does not use an SVG `rel=icon` for a tab, and a browser with no
      usable declared icon falls back to the root path — which was answering
      with the site's own 404 page, 49,969 bytes of HTML. The ICO is listed
      FIRST and the SVG second: anything that understands SVG prefers it on
      type, and anything that does not now has a real file to find.

      The other conventional paths (/apple-touch-icon.png and its precomposed
      twin, /icon-512.png, /favicon.svg) are shipped in public/ without being
      declared here, because the software that wants them does not read this
      head at all.
    */}
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32" />
    <link rel="icon" href={faviconUrl} type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href={appleTouchIconUrl} />
    <link rel="manifest" href="/site.webmanifest" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content={theme === "dark" ? "#0A0A0A" : "#FAFAF8"} />

    {/*
      Pre-launch: keep an unfinished site carrying {PLACEHOLDER} copy out of
      search results. Follows one switch, PUBLIC_ALLOW_INDEXING, so launch is a
      configuration change rather than an edit to every page. The second lock is
      the X-Robots-Tag header in public/_headers, removed by hand at launch.
      DESIGN-PLAN section 16.
    */}
    {!allowIndexing && <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />}
    {canonical && <link rel="canonical" href={canonical.href} />}
    {
      Astro.site &&
        LOCALES.map((code) => (
          <link rel="alternate" hreflang={code} href={new URL(switchLocalePath(pathname, code), Astro.site).href} />
        ))
    }
    {Astro.site && <link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href={new URL(localePath("en"), Astro.site).href} />}

    {/*
      Sharing metadata. The image is always one of the owner's photographs, from
      the page it belongs to. There is no generated card and no invented
      artwork. Description is present only where the content supplies one:
      inventing brand copy for a meta tag is still inventing brand copy.
    */}
    <meta property="og:site_name" content={brand} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={documentTitle} />
    <meta property="og:locale" content={locale === "it" ? "it_IT" : "en_GB"} />
    {canonical && <meta property="og:url" content={canonical.href} />}
    {description && <meta property="og:description" content={description} />}
    {/*
      EVERY page gets a share image, not just the ones that thought to pass one.
      Traffic arrives from Instagram, so a shared link IS the first impression,
      and a link with no card is a worse first impression than any photograph.
      Falls back to the arrival, which is the drop the brand is currently
      showing, so the card changes when he changes that one field.
    */}
    {shareImage && <meta property="og:image" content={shareImage} />}
    {shareImage && <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />}
    {shareImage && <meta name="twitter:image" content={shareImage} />}
    <meta name="twitter:card" content={image ? "summary_large_image" : "summary"} />
    {/*
      ONE PRELOAD, AND THE SECOND FONT IS A KNOWN TRADE (corrected 2026-08-11,
      section 84). This said "Mono is not on the hero" and that has been false
      since the price moved onto the first screen: the home arrival prints
      `priceFrom` in mono and every Creature page prints its price in mono over
      the opening photograph. Mono is on the LCP screen of the two most-entered
      pages in the site.

      It is deliberately still not preloaded. The file is 37KB against Archivo's
      89KB, and on the Slow 4G profile the arrival photograph is already the
      long pole (photo ready 3512ms, section 79). `font-display: swap` means the
      price paints immediately in the system monospace and re-sets when the file
      lands — a short line, one shift, no blank text — where a second preload
      would put 37KB in front of the only image that must not be delayed.

      What must NOT happen is this comment going stale again: if a third
      register reaches a first screen, measure before adding a link.
    */}
    <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/archivo-latin-var.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />

    {/*
      DOES THIS BROWSER RUN SCRIPTS (2026-08-16, section 129). One class, set
      before anything paints, and the only thing on the site that depends on it
      is the cart — which cannot exist without a script, because a cart is state
      that has to survive navigation and this site is static HTML on a CDN.

      IN THE HEAD, DELIBERATELY. The controls it reveals are `display: none`
      until this runs, so a reader with scripts ON must never see them flash in
      after the page has painted, and a reader with scripts OFF must never see
      them at all. That ordering is the whole reason this is not at the foot of
      the body with everything else.

      It says nothing about the rest of the site. Every other page renders,
      navigates and posts with this class absent, and nothing may start
      depending on it without saying so on the page it affects.
    */}
    <script is:inline>document.documentElement.classList.add("js");</script>
  </head>
  <body>
    {/* Label, not mono: this is a control, and mono is data only (tokens.css). */}
    <a
      href="#content"
      class="type-label text-label sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:left-content focus:top-u1 focus:z-10 focus:bg-bg focus:p-u1 focus:text-fg"
    >
      {t(locale, "skipToContent")}
    </a>

    {/*
      No spine at this level. The spine belongs to text surfaces only and stops
      at the edge of any full-bleed media (DESIGN-PLAN section 2). Header,
      footer and every text section draw their own segment at the same offset,
      so the line reads continuous and breaks exactly where a photograph starts.
    */}
    {/*
      THE SHIPPING LINE (2026-08-04). One thin line at the top of every page:
      his real policy, in his own words (`shippingFree`, unmarked because both
      the fact and the wording are his). His other shop runs exactly this banner
      over the same photography and it reads as a shop within a second, which is
      what this site was failing to do.

      STATIC, not fixed: it scrolls away with the page, so it says its one thing
      at arrival and then leaves the photography alone. It is not the persistent
      bar section 4 removed, because it does not persist.
    */}
    {/*
      AND IT STANDS DOWN WHERE THE CHROME FLOATS (2026-08-12, section 115).

      PIXEL ZERO IS THE WHOLE POINT of the float, and a paper strap above the
      chrome moves the photograph down by exactly as much as the band used to.
      Rick Owens has nothing above his hero; Westwood has nothing above hers.

      WHAT IT COSTS IS LESS THAN IT LOOKS, and this was checked rather than
      assumed: the banner prints `shippingFree`, and SiteFooter prints the same
      `shippingFree` string from the same field on every page (section 108). So
      on a page that opens on a photograph, his sentence was appearing TWICE,
      and what stands down here is the copy of it, not the sentence.

      IT IS OURS AND IT IS A TRADE. He chose the banner's placement when the
      site had a band there anyway; this moves his line from the first screen to
      the last one on five pages. /process and /contact never carried it
      (`shopChrome={false}`) and the catalogue and order pages, which do not open
      on a photograph, keep it exactly where it was.
    */}
    {banner && shopChrome && !chromeOver && (
      <div class="bg-bg text-fg hairline-b frame flex justify-center py-u1">
        <p class="type-label text-label banner-line text-center">{banner}</p>
      </div>
    )}
    <SiteHeader locale={locale} pageTheme={theme} over={chromeOver} />

    <div class="flex min-h-svh flex-col">
      <main id="content" class="flex-1">
        <slot />
      </main>
      <SiteFooter locale={locale} settings={settings} />
    </div>

    {/*
      THE CHROME OBSERVER IS GONE (2026-08-11, section 87), and this is the
      cheapest thing the band bought. It watched every media surface pass under
      the fixed marks and swapped their polarity to whichever value had been
      measured for that photograph. It was correct, it was cheap, it ran on
      every page — and it could not solve the problem, because 71 of 97
      placements had no legible side to swap TO. The band removed the question
      instead of answering it: the marks sit on paper and never cross a
      photograph at all.
    */}

    {/*
      THE LOOPS (2026-08-11, section 83). Marked live only when JavaScript runs
      AND reduced motion is off, exactly as the reveal and the wash are, so the
      still is what a reader gets in every other case.

      Each loop plays only while it is on screen. A page carrying several would
      otherwise hold several decoders open on a phone, which is the cost that
      makes video expensive rather than the file size.
    */}
    {/*
      THE CART, ON EVERY PAGE (2026-08-16, section 129).

      TWO JOBS, and they are on every page because the count is: paint the
      number in the chrome, and let a piece be added from its own page without
      leaving it. The cart PAGE has its own script, which is the only place the
      list is read and written line by line.

      IT IS NOT AN ENHANCEMENT AND DOES NOT PRETEND TO BE. A cart is
      per-visitor state that has to survive navigation, and this site is static
      HTML on a CDN: no session, no per-request render, so localStorage is the
      only way to hold it. The controls this drives are hidden until the
      document carries `js`, and the reasoning is written out in
      src/pages/[lang]/cart.astro rather than hidden here.

      IT CHANGES NOTHING ELSE. Every other page works with this script absent,
      exactly as it did before a cart existed.
    */}
    <script is:inline>
      (() => {
        const KEY = "ac-cart";
        const read = () => {
          try {
            const v = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) || "{}");
            return v && typeof v === "object" ? v : {};
          } catch {
            return {};
          }
        };

        /*
          PIECES, NOT LINES. Two of one Creature is two pieces in the chrome,
          which is what "how many pieces are in it" means to the person reading
          it and what the confirmation email counts.
        */
        const count = () =>
          Object.values(read()).reduce((n, e) => n + (Number(e && e.qty) || 0), 0);

        const badge = document.querySelector("[data-cart-count]");
        const paint = () => {
          if (!badge) return;
          const n = count();
          badge.textContent = n > 0 ? String(n) : "";
          badge.hidden = n <= 0;
        };
        /* The cart page and this file both write; either one repaints both. */
        document.addEventListener("ac-cart-changed", paint);
        /* Another tab of the same site is the same cart. */
        window.addEventListener("storage", (event) => {
          if (event.key === KEY) paint();
        });
        paint();

        for (const button of document.querySelectorAll("[data-add-to-cart]")) {
          /*
            THE BUTTON'S OWN WORD, READ OFF THE BUTTON (2026-08-16). This was a
            `data-add-label` attribute, and it was deleted by accident while
            tidying a comment out of the same tag — after which the label below
            never went back, SILENTLY, because a missing dataset key is
            `undefined` rather than an error. Found by looking at the published
            page rather than at the code, which is the only thing that finds it.
            There is nothing to keep in step now.
          */
          const label = button.textContent;
          button.addEventListener("click", () => {
            const slug = button.dataset.addToCart;
            const block = button.closest(".cart-add");
            const radios = block ? block.querySelectorAll("input[type=radio]") : [];
            const size = block?.querySelector("input[type=radio]:checked")?.value;
            /*
              A PIECE WITH SIZES IS NOT ADDED WITHOUT ONE (section 130). Nothing
              is pre-checked, so pressing ADD before choosing has to say so
              rather than pick the first size on the reader's behalf. The button
              names what is missing and the focus moves to the choice, which is
              the whole of the correction — no colour, no box, nothing new.
            */
            if (radios.length > 0 && !size) {
              button.textContent = button.dataset.needsSize || label;
              clearTimeout(button._ackTimer);
              button._ackTimer = setTimeout(() => {
                button.textContent = label;
              }, 2400);
              radios[0].focus();
              return;
            }
            const cart = read();
            const entry = cart[slug] || {qty: 0};
            entry.qty = (Number(entry.qty) || 0) + 1;
            /*
              ONE LINE PER PIECE. Adding the same Creature in another size
              raises the quantity and takes the size just chosen, rather than
              opening a second line: the wire format the endpoint validates is
              one quantity and one size per slug. The cart page shows the line,
              so the answer is never hidden from the person who chose it.
            */
            if (size) entry.size = size;
            cart[slug] = entry;
            try {
              localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(cart));
            } catch {
              /*
                Private mode, or a full store. A cart cannot exist here, and
                saying nothing would be the one unacceptable outcome: the reader
                presses a button, the label says it went in, and nothing did.
                So they go to this piece's own order form, which needs no
                storage and no script and can still sell them the thing they
                just asked for.
              */
              if (button.dataset.fallbackHref) window.location.href = button.dataset.fallbackHref;
              return;
            }
            /*
              THE LABEL CONFIRMS THE ACT AND THEN GOES BACK TO NAMING IT. It
              said "In the cart" and stayed that way, so a control that still
              added one more on every press was labelled as a state — press it
              twice and it reads as though nothing happened, while the count
              quietly went to two. The count in the chrome is the state; this is
              the acknowledgement, and it belongs to the press rather than to
              the cart.
            */
            button.textContent = button.dataset.addedLabel || label;
            clearTimeout(button._ackTimer);
            button._ackTimer = setTimeout(() => {
              button.textContent = label;
            }, 2400);
            paint();
          });
        }
      })();
    </script>

    <script is:inline>
      (() => {
        const loops = document.querySelectorAll("video.video-loop");
        if (!loops.length) return;
        if (!("IntersectionObserver" in window)) return;
        if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) return;

        document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-loops-live", "");

        /*
          A CLIP THAT PLAYS ONCE HAS TO BE LET GO OF (2026-08-17, section 124).

          Dropping `loop` is not enough on its own. This observer plays whatever
          is on screen and pauses whatever leaves, and after a video has ENDED
          its position sits at the end — so the next time it scrolls back into
          view, `play()` rewinds it to zero and runs it again. The reader would
          get the arrival once per visit to the top of the page, which is a
          loop with extra steps.

          So a `data-play-once` clip is started on its first intersection and
          then unobserved: never paused, never resumed, never rewound. It ends
          on its last frame and that frame is what stays.
        */
        const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries) => {
            for (const entry of entries) {
              const video = entry.target;
              if (video.hasAttribute("data-play-once")) {
                if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
                observer.unobserve(video);
                video.play().catch(() => {});
                continue;
              }
              if (entry.isIntersecting) video.play().catch(() => {});
              else video.pause();
            }
          },
          {rootMargin: "10% 0px"},
        );
        loops.forEach((video) => observer.observe(video));
      })();
    </script>

    {/*
      THE PHOTOGRAPHY REVEAL (DESIGN-PLAN section 24). A presence arriving: the
      frame rises a little and resolves, once, and is never touched again.

      Two refusals are the important part of this script.

      IT MARKS THE ROOT BEFORE HIDING ANYTHING. The hidden state lives behind
      data-reveal-live, so a reader with JavaScript off, or with a blocked
      script, or on a browser without IntersectionObserver, gets every
      photograph visible. On a site that is entirely photographs, the failure
      mode of getting this wrong is a blank page, so it is not left to chance.

      IT UNOBSERVES ON FIRST SIGHT. A frame reveals once and never re-hides on
      the way back up. Motion that repeats every time a reader scrolls past is
      the "elements bouncing" this is meant to avoid.

      Reduced motion: the root is never marked, so nothing is ever hidden and no
      transition is ever declared. The CSS repeats the guarantee in case the
      preference changes after load.
    */}
    <script is:inline>
      (() => {
        const frames = document.querySelectorAll("[data-reveal]");
        if (!frames.length) return;
        if (!("IntersectionObserver" in window)) return;
        if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) return;

        document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reveal-live", "");

        const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries) => {
            for (const entry of entries) {
              if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
              entry.target.setAttribute("data-revealed", "");
              observer.unobserve(entry.target);
            }
          },
          // A little into the viewport, so a frame is already resolving by the
          // time it is properly on screen rather than starting at the edge.
          {rootMargin: "0px 0px -8% 0px"},
        );

        frames.forEach((frame) => observer.observe(frame));
      })();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
